Ramiel.wikitext:
{{DarkAngelsPortal}}
{{spoiler|source=Reparation (Short Story)}}
'''Ramiel''' is an [[Interrogator Chaplain]] of the [[Dark Angels]] [[Chapter]].{{Fn|1}}


==Biography==
Before attaining his current position he served under [[Space Marine Sergeant|Sergeant]] [[Sariel (Dark Angels)|Sariel]], who saved Ramiel's life on the planet [[Tervanaous IV]] as the Dark Angels fought the [[Tyranids]] invading the planet. Ramiel would learn much from Sariel, before he joined the [[Deathwatch]], and still reflected on the Sergeant's words years after he became an Interrogator Chaplain. It was during his time as a Chaplain, that the Dark Angels' inner circle learned that a member of the Fallen had been captured by the [[Kabal of the Bladed Lotus]] and brought to the planet [[Damorragh]] to compete in the [[Dark Eldar]]s' arena games.{{Fn|1}}   

Knowing his duty to the Chapter, Ramiel created the identity of [[Thorolf Icewalkdr]], of the [[Space Wolves]] and allowed himself to be captured by the Kabal. There on Damorragh, Ramiel would fight several battles in the arenas before the target of his mission presented itself. Calling himself [[Ecanus (Fallen Angel)|Ecanus]], the Fallen identified himself as a Dark Angel when he was thrown inside the same cell as Ramiel. As Ecanus expressed his displeasure at sharing the same cell as a Space Wolf, Ramiel kept up the charade until the right moment presented itself. This would continue for some time, as each fought in the Kabal's latest tournament, until the Dark Eldar decided to have them team up and fight as one. Still disguising himself as Thorolf, Ramiel and Ecanus worked well together and eventually won the tournament by killing the arena's champion, the [[Wych]] [[Khalys Dzhar]]. It was after her death that Ramiel threw off his disguise and confronted Ecanus.{{Fn|1}}   

As Ecanus looked on in shock, Ramiel gave him his true name and offered the Fallen a chance at redemption. In a rage at this revelation, Ecanus denounced Ramiel as a pawn of the [[Lion El'Jonson|Lion]] and attacked the Chaplain. They fought each other in the arena, as the Dark Eldar cheered them on and the more experienced Ecanus soon gained the upper hand over Ramiel. As the Chaplain wavered under the assault, he reflected back to the lessons Sariel had taught him about fighting a stronger opponent; and weakly swung his sword at the Fallen, which allowed Ecancus to launch a strike that would kill him. Ramiel predicted the attack however and dodged the strike, before he stabbed a surprised Ecanus in his primary heart. Weakened by the wound, Ecanus struggled with Ramiel as the Chaplain pulled his blade out of the Fallen's body, tearing his second heart in the process. Killed instantly, Ecanus' body fell in a ruined heap in front of Ramiel, as roars of approval from the Dark Eldar filled the arena.{{Fn|1}}  

As the crowd roared their approval the [[Orator]], a Dark Eldar that served as a narrator for Damorragh’s arenas, told Ramiel what his prize would be for winning the tournament. He would join [[K’shaic]], the Kabal's [[Archon]], to the depths of Commorragh, where he would fight in even greater arenas. With the Fallen dead, Ramiel had no intention of fighting for the Dark Eldars' pleasure any longer; and as K'shaic looked down upon Ramiel from his seat in the arena, the Chaplain grabbed the weapon dropped by Ecanus and threw it at the Archon with all his might. The blow flew true and pierced K'shaic's chest, but a burst of light emerged from the Archon, when the weapon hit him. When it cleared, Ramiel saw that the Archon had used a displacement field to swap places with the Orator, who fell to the ground with a shocked expression on his face. K'shaic gave the Dark Angel a nod of mock respect, as the Orator died and Ramiel could do nothing but give a final prayer to the [[Emperor]] and await his fate.{{Fn|1}}

==Trivia==
{{Trivia}}
The name ''Ramiel'' might be inspired by [[Wikipedia:Ramiel|Ramiel]], either one of 20 fallen [[Wikipedia:Watcher (angel)|Watchers]] (angels) who sinned and rebelled against God by mating with human women and creating an offspring called [[Wikipedia:Nephilim|Nephilim]]. Or alternatively an archangel which is normally spelled as ''R'''e'''miel'' but sometimes also as ''R'''a'''miel''. Both spellings translate as "thunder of God".


==Sources==
*{{Endn|1}}: [[Reparation (Short Story)]] — [[Hammer and Bolter 13]], pgs. 24-43

